::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which planning move best fits a professional audience?
{
~A. Use casual slang before identifying the audience.#This ignores context. Professional communication starts by matching purpose, audience, and tone.
=B. Identify audience, purpose, context, and needed tone before drafting.#Correct. These choices guide the rest of the professional communication plan.
~C. Choose the longest format available.#Length does not equal quality. The format should fit audience and purpose.
~D. Avoid revising after feedback.#Professional communication improves through revision and audience awareness.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which interview response shows listening and collaboration?
{
~A. Ignore the question and talk about a different topic.#This does not show listening. A strong response connects to the question or speaker.
~B. Interrupt with an unrelated story.#This weakens collaboration because it shifts attention away from the speaker's point.
~C. Repeat your opinion without acknowledging the other person.#This does not build from the conversation. Collaboration requires response to others.
=D. Paraphrase the point and ask a relevant follow-up question.#Correct. This shows understanding and keeps the discussion moving productively.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which plan best supports clear speaking?
{
=A. Organize the message with purpose, main points, transitions, and audience examples.#Correct. This plan gives the audience a clear path through the spoken message.
~B. Memorize random facts without structure.#Facts need organization to become clear communication.
~C. Remove transitions so the speech is shorter.#Transitions help the audience follow the message.
~D. Use the same examples for every audience.#Examples should fit the audience's knowledge and needs.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which discussion move best supports a professional group decision?
{
~A. Dismiss every idea before hearing the explanation.#This shuts down collaboration. Professional discussion requires listening and reasoning.
~B. Let one person speak for the whole group without input.#This limits collaboration and may miss useful perspectives.
=C. Summarize options, ask for evidence, and invite responses.#Correct. This helps the group compare ideas and move toward a reasoned decision.
~D. Change the topic whenever disagreement begins.#Disagreement can be useful when handled respectfully and with evidence.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which response best handles disagreement?
{
~A. Attack the person instead of the idea.#This is not respectful communication. Focus on ideas, evidence, and reasoning.
~B. Refuse to explain your point.#This prevents productive discussion. A clear response includes reasoning.
=C. Acknowledge the idea, explain your concern, and offer evidence.#Correct. This keeps disagreement respectful and focused on communication goals.
~D. Pretend everyone agrees.#Avoiding disagreement can leave problems unresolved.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.01
Question:
Which multimedia plan is strongest?
{
=A. Use relevant visuals, clear headings, and speaker notes aligned to each main point.#Correct. Multimedia should support the spoken message and audience understanding.
~B. Put every sentence on the slides.#This overloads the audience and reduces the speaker's role.
~C. Add images that do not connect to the topic.#Unrelated images distract from the communication purpose.
~D. Choose media before knowing the audience.#Audience and purpose should guide media choices.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which digital publication is most audience-aware?
{
~A. A post with no date, purpose, or next step.#This fails the audience because key information is missing.
~B. A design with tiny low-contrast text.#This weakens accessibility and usability.
~C. A decorative image without explanation.#Decoration alone does not communicate the needed message.
=D. A clear announcement with headings, readable text, and a visible action step.#Correct. This design helps the audience understand and use the message.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
How should visuals support a professional briefing?
{
~A. They should replace the speaker's explanation.#Visuals support the speaker but do not replace spoken reasoning.
=B. They should guide attention to evidence and main ideas the speaker explains.#Correct. Visuals and spoken explanation should work together.
~C. They should be unrelated to keep the audience entertained.#Unrelated visuals distract from the message.
~D. They should hide the recommendation.#A briefing should make the recommendation clear, not hidden.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.01
Question:
Which briefing choice uses research effectively?
{
~A. State a recommendation without any support.#This weakens the briefing because the audience cannot see the reasoning.
~B. List sources without explaining them.#A source list does not show how evidence supports the message.
=C. Use relevant source evidence and explain how it supports the recommendation.#Correct. Research-supported communication connects evidence to audience understanding.
~D. Use only personal preference.#Preference alone is not research support.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.02
Question:
What should come first when explaining a source?
{
=A. Identify the evidence the source actually provides.#Correct. Accurate evidence is the foundation for inference and explanation.
~B. Decide what you wish the source proved.#This can distort the source. Evidence should be read accurately first.
~C. Skip the source and state the conclusion.#This removes support and weakens credibility.
~D. Add a dramatic phrase before checking accuracy.#Drama does not replace accurate source use.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.03
Question:
Which statement is a careful inference?
{
~A. This small survey proves all audiences think the same way.#This overclaims. A small survey cannot prove a universal conclusion.
=B. This survey suggests this audience may prefer a clearer digital announcement.#Correct. This inference stays close to the evidence and respects limits.
~C. This source means evidence is never needed again.#This contradicts responsible source use.
~D. This example proves every message must be a slideshow.#This overextends the source beyond its support.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.04
Question:
Which sentence best explains source evidence?
{
~A. This source is good because I like it.#This is preference, not explanation. The audience needs to know what the evidence shows.
~B. The source is included because the assignment asked for one.#This explains compliance, not communication value.
=C. The source supports the recommendation by showing that complete reminders helped members know when and where to attend.#Correct. This connects evidence to the recommendation.
~D. The source proves everything about the topic.#This overclaims and can mislead the audience.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which choice best adapts a message for families?
{
~A. Use class-only abbreviations without explanation.#This may confuse families who do not know the abbreviations.
~B. Use inside jokes as the main explanation.#Inside jokes can exclude or confuse the audience.
~C. Hide the main information at the end.#This makes the message harder to use.
=D. Use a respectful tone with clear date, location, purpose, and contact information.#Correct. This adapts language and details to the audience's needs.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.01
Question:
What should a portfolio introduction explain?
{
=A. Purpose, audience, organization, and skills demonstrated.#Correct. This orients the reviewer and shows that the portfolio is purposeful.
~B. Only the number of files included.#Quantity does not explain communication value.
~C. Only how difficult the work was.#Effort may be mentioned, but the introduction should focus on communication evidence.
~D. Nothing, because artifacts explain themselves.#Artifacts need reflection so the reviewer understands what they prove.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which portfolio organization is strongest?
{
~A. Random file order with no labels.#Random order makes the portfolio difficult to evaluate.
~B. Decorative pages first, then unlabeled work.#Decoration does not replace organization or explanation.
=C. Group artifacts by skill and include a reflection for each one.#Correct. This helps the reviewer connect artifacts to communication standards.
~D. Put all reflections in a separate unlabeled file.#This separates evidence from explanation and creates confusion.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which tone best fits a professional briefing to school staff?
{
~A. Sarcastic and dismissive.#This tone is not appropriate for a professional audience.
~B. Careless and overly casual.#This weakens professionalism and may reduce trust.
~C. Secretive and vague.#A briefing should make the message clear.
=D. Respectful, clear, and evidence-focused.#Correct. This tone supports credibility and audience understanding.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which interview answer is strongest?
{
=A. It directly answers the question, gives a relevant example, and uses professional tone.#Correct. This response supports purpose, audience, and clarity.
~B. It avoids the question completely.#Avoiding the question weakens communication and does not meet the interview purpose.
~C. It uses slang and no example.#This may not fit a professional interview and lacks support.
~D. It criticizes the interviewer.#This is not appropriate professional communication.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which speaking plan best supports audience understanding?
{
~A. Begin with unrelated details.#This makes the message harder to follow.
~B. Use examples the audience cannot connect to.#Examples should fit the audience's knowledge and needs.
~C. Skip transitions to save time.#Transitions guide listeners from one idea to the next.
=D. Organize ideas with an opening, main points, evidence, and closing.#Correct. This structure helps listeners understand and remember the message.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which group discussion choice best supports collaboration?
{
=A. Invite each member to contribute and connect ideas to the task.#Correct. This keeps discussion inclusive and focused on the communication goal.
~B. Let one person decide without discussion.#This does not support collaboration.
~C. Ignore quieter members.#This may cause the group to miss useful perspectives.
~D. Focus on unrelated topics.#This prevents the group from completing the communication task.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
What is the best response to a teammate's weak idea?
{
~A. Say the idea is bad and move on.#This attacks the idea without helping the group improve it.
~B. Ignore the teammate for the rest of the discussion.#This damages collaboration and respect.
=C. Ask a clarifying question and suggest a way to connect the idea to the goal.#Correct. This keeps the discussion respectful and productive.
~D. Change the goal so no one disagrees.#Avoiding disagreement does not improve the idea or the task.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.01
Question:
Which slide choice best supports multimedia planning?
{
~A. One crowded slide with every detail.#This overloads the audience because listeners cannot easily identify the main idea or supporting points.
~B. Random animations on every point.#Too many animations distract from the message and can make the audience miss the main point.
~C. Slides with no connection to the speech.#This creates confusion because the visual support does not match what the speaker is saying.
=D. Clear slides with one main idea, relevant visuals, and notes for the speaker.#Correct. This supports both audience understanding and speaker delivery.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which digital design choice best improves usability?
{
~A. Hide the sign-up link in small text.#This makes the action step hard to find.
~B. Use only decoration with no message.#Decoration alone does not communicate.
~C. Remove all headings.#Headings help the audience scan and understand structure.
=D. Use readable contrast, clear headings, and a visible next step.#Correct. These choices help the audience understand and act.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Why should speaker notes match slide content?
{
~A. To make the speech longer.#Length is not the purpose of speaker notes.
~B. To avoid explaining evidence.#Speaker notes should help the speaker explain evidence clearly.
=C. To help the speaker explain visuals in a way that supports the audience's understanding.#Correct. Notes and visuals should work together to support the presentation.
~D. To replace audience awareness.#Speaker notes should include audience awareness, not replace it.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.01
Question:
Which research question best guides a professional briefing?
{
~A. Why is everything confusing?#This is too vague and emotional to guide useful research.
~B. Can I avoid using sources?#This does not support research-based communication.
~C. What do I already believe about announcements?#This starts with personal belief instead of inquiry.
=D. What communication practices help students and families receive school-event information clearly?#Correct. This question is specific, researchable, and connected to a professional communication problem.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.02
Question:
Which evidence statement is most precise?
{
=A. In the survey, 32 of 40 students said reminders with the date and location were easier to use.#Correct. This gives the source context and specific information accurately.
~B. Students like reminders.#This is too vague and lacks the source detail.
~C. Reminders are always perfect.#This overclaims and is not precise evidence.
~D. Everyone needs more messages.#This is broad and unsupported by the source detail.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q26::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q26
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.03
Question:
Which inference avoids overclaiming?
{
~A. Clear reminders guarantee attendance.#This overclaims. Reminders may help but cannot guarantee attendance.
~B. Families do not need event details.#This contradicts the communication purpose.
~C. Students never read reminders.#This is not supported by the evidence.
=D. Clear reminders may help this audience find and use event information.#Correct. This inference stays close to the evidence and audience context.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q27::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q27
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.04
Question:
Which explanation best connects evidence to recommendation?
{
~A. I recommend reminders because I like reminders.#This is preference, not source explanation.
~B. The source exists, so the recommendation is correct.#The speaker must explain how the evidence supports the recommendation.
=C. The evidence shows that complete reminders were easier to use, so the plan should include clear date and location details.#Correct. This connects source evidence to a practical communication recommendation.
~D. The recommendation needs no support.#Professional recommendations are stronger with evidence and explanation.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q28::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q28
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.03
Question:
Which message is most audience-aware for students?
{
~A. Use unclear formal wording and no action step.#Students need clear information and a next step.
~B. Hide the purpose until the end.#Audience-aware messages state the purpose clearly.
~C. Use only staff policy language.#This may not fit student understanding or motivation.
=D. Use concise language, clear benefits, and a visible action step.#Correct. This matches the audience's need for quick, usable information.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q29::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q29
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.01
Question:
Which artifact explanation is strongest?
{
=A. This outline shows how I organized a professional speech for a specific audience using evidence and transitions.#Correct. The explanation names the artifact, skill, audience, and communication choices.
~B. This file is here because it was finished.#Completion alone does not show communication mastery.
~C. This artifact took a long time.#Effort alone is not enough. The reviewer needs the skill shown.
~D. This is probably my best work.#This is vague and does not explain what the artifact demonstrates.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q30::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q30
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.02
Question:
Which portfolio flow is easiest for a reviewer?
{
~A. Unlabeled artifacts in random order.#This creates confusion for the reviewer.
=B. Introduction, labeled skill sections, artifact reflections, and closing reflection.#Correct. This gives the reviewer a clear path through the portfolio.
~C. Reflections separated from artifacts with no labels.#This makes connections harder to follow.
~D. No organization because the reviewer can decide.#A portfolio should communicate clearly, not make the reviewer guess.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q31::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q31
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which revision improves professional tone?
{
~A. Replace clear wording with sarcasm.#Sarcasm can weaken professionalism and confuse the audience.
~B. Remove specific details.#Professional tone still needs clear details.
=C. Replace vague casual wording with respectful, specific language.#Correct. This supports credibility and audience understanding.
~D. Add unrelated jokes.#Jokes may distract from the professional purpose.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q32::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q32
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which listening move helps during a professional discussion?
{
~A. Plan your next comment while ignoring the speaker.#This prevents accurate understanding.
=B. Restate the main idea before adding your response.#Correct. Restating confirms understanding and helps the discussion stay connected.
~C. Interrupt with a new topic.#This breaks the flow of communication.
~D. Assume the speaker is wrong without listening.#This blocks productive discussion.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q33::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q33
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which delivery choice supports clarity?
{
=A. Use a pace, volume, and organization the audience can follow.#Correct. Delivery choices should help the audience understand the message.
~B. Speak as quickly as possible.#Speaking too quickly can reduce understanding.
~C. Avoid emphasizing key points.#Emphasis helps listeners identify important ideas.
~D. Ignore audience confusion.#Speakers should adjust when the audience needs support.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q34::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q34
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which collaboration choice best helps a team prepare a briefing?
{
~A. Divide tasks without sharing updates.#This can create disconnected work.
=B. Share progress, compare evidence, and agree on the main message.#Correct. Collaboration requires coordination and shared understanding.
~C. Let one person hide all sources.#This weakens transparency and teamwork.
~D. Avoid discussing the audience.#Audience awareness is essential to the briefing.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q35::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q35
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which response shows respectful disagreement?
{
~A. Your idea makes no sense.#This attacks the person or idea without useful reasoning.
~B. I refuse to explain my concern.#This prevents productive discussion.
=C. I see your point, but the evidence may support a different recommendation.#Correct. This acknowledges the idea and gives a reasoned disagreement.
~D. Everyone should ignore that idea.#This shuts down collaboration.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q36::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q36
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.01
Question:
Which choice best connects multimedia to audience needs?
{
~A. Add a chart because charts look advanced.#This focuses on appearance, not audience need.
=B. Use a chart only when it clarifies evidence the audience needs to understand.#Correct. Multimedia should support clarity and purpose.
~C. Use every media type available.#Too many media elements can distract from the message.
~D. Avoid explaining visuals.#The speaker should explain visuals so the audience understands them.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q37::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q37
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.DIG.01
Question:
Which final check best improves a digital publication?
{
=A. Check whether the audience can quickly find the purpose, key details, and next step.#Correct. This check focuses on usability and communication purpose.
~B. Check whether the publication uses the most colors.#Color use matters only if it supports readability and clarity.
~C. Check whether the message is hidden behind images.#Important information should be visible and readable.
~D. Check whether the publication is longer than necessary.#Longer is not automatically clearer or more useful.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q38::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q38
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which slide revision best supports audience understanding?
{
~A. Add more text until the slide is full.#Crowded slides can overload the audience.
=B. Replace a crowded paragraph with a clear heading and three key points.#Correct. This supports scanning and lets the speaker explain details aloud.
~C. Remove the main idea.#The audience needs the main idea to follow the presentation.
~D. Add unrelated graphics.#Unrelated graphics distract from the communication purpose.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q39::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q39
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.RES.01
Question:
Which source choice is best for a professional recommendation?
{
=A. A source that directly relates to the problem and provides useful evidence.#Correct. Relevant evidence helps support the recommendation and audience reasoning.
~B. A source chosen only because it has a long title.#A long title does not prove relevance or usefulness.
~C. A source unrelated to the audience's problem.#Unrelated sources weaken the recommendation.
~D. A source that the speaker cannot explain.#If the speaker cannot explain it, the source will not help the audience.
}
::SPEECH_U06_UA_Q40::
Question ID: SPEECH_U06_UA_Q40
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which complete plan best shows Unit 6 mastery?
{
~A. One generic message with no evidence, no audience adaptation, and no clear format.#This misses the key Unit 6 skills.
=B. A plan that identifies audience needs, uses evidence, chooses presentation and digital formats, and explains communication choices.#Correct. This combines professional purpose, research, speaking, presentation, and digital communication.
~C. A colorful design with no source support or purpose.#Visual appeal alone does not show mastery.
~D. A speech outline that ignores audience, tone, and format.#A complete plan must connect organization to audience and purpose.
}
